## 1. Introduction
Metal-organic framework materials (MOFs) are a kind of porous materials formed by self-supporting metal ions or clusters and organic ligands (He, Zhou, Yildirim & Chen, 2013; Long & Yaghi, 2009; Zhou & Kitagawa, 2014). Due to their large specific surface areas, high and tunable nanoscale pore structures, unsaturated metal sites, and chemical modification (Farha et al., 2012; He, Zhou, Yildirim & Chen, 2013; Liu et al., 2014; Xue et al., 2013; Zhao, Bu, Zhai, Tran & Feng, 2015), MOFs have shown great potential in various applications including gas separation and storage, chemical sensing, catalysis (Chen, Gu, Mirza, Zhang & Zhang, 2018; Jiao, Wang, Jiang & Xu, 2017; Li et al., 2018; Yang et al., 2015; Yi et al., 2017). However, due to the crystallization of MOF, they are most commonly found in small sized powders, which lead to easy agglomeration and are not readily recyclable. In addition, most MOFs have poor stability in water, which could greatly limit their application in water treatment (Burtch, Jasuja & Walton, 2014; Fu, Xu & Zhang, 2014; Furukawa, Cordova, O'Keeffe & Yaghi, 2013; Guillerm et al., 2012; Kandiah et al., 2010; Lammert, Bernt, Vermoortele, De Vos & Stock, 2013). Therefore, process ability, handling, and recycling of MOFs are still remaining as a significant challenge (Furukawa, Reboul, Diring, Sumida & Kitagawa, 2014; Zhu, Yang, Cranston & Zhu, 2016).
Meanwhile, in order to overcome these above limitation, the functionalized of composite materials by using appropriate substrates to support/modulate the growth of MOFs is an effective way to realize the recovery and reuse of functional MOFs. In general, the process requires surface modification to enhance the compatibility between the MOFs and substrates, and the use of restrictive synthesis conditions ensures that the substrates are stable during processing (Bétard & Fischer, 2012; Ke et al., 2011; Qiu, Xue & Zhu, 2014; Zhu et al., 2012; Zhu, Yang, Cranston & Zhu, 2016; Zou, Yu, Li & Li, 2013). It has been demonstrated that MOFs particles are incorporated into the polymer or fibrous substrate by blending, deposition or in situ growth (Rose, Böhringer, Jolly, Fischer & Kaskel, 2011; Schwab et al., 2008; Wisser et al., 2015). For example: Yang et al. used the method of in situ growth to grow MOFs on carbon fibers (CF) to increase the tensile strength of CF (Yang, Jiang, Huang, Guo & Shao, 2017).
Cellulose aerogels (CA) exhibit great potential as substrates because of their high strength, lightweight, low cost, water stability, flexibility, non-toxic and excellent processability (Arnaud et al., 2015; Liao et al., 2016; Cai et al., 2012; Liebner, Potthast, Rosenau, Haimer & Wendland, 2008; Litschauer et al., 2010; Liu, Yan, Tao, Yu & Liu, 2012; Sehaqui, Zhou & Berglund, 2011;). The dispersion of MOF on the cellulose surfaces is being successfully prepared to produce MOF based composite materials with new functional performance (Ren, Gao & Lei, 2018). Zhu et al. successfully prepared $50\mathrm{\mt}\%$ of metal-organic frameworks $@$ cellulose nanocrystals composites, the resulting composites showed good application in water treatment (Zhu, Yang, Cranston & Zhu, 2016). Maatar and Boufi used Fenton’s reagent to prepare A poly (methacrylic acid-co-maleic acid) grafted nanofibrillated cellulose (NFC-MAA-MA) aerogels by radical polymerization in an aqueous solution, then used as an adsorbent to remove heavy metals from aqueous solutions. NFC-MAA-MA has a high adsorption capacity for $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and the washed aerogel can be reused without significant loss of adsorption capacity. By using EDTA as a desorption solution more than $98\%$ of the adsorbed metal ions can be recovered (Maatar & Boufi, 2015). It can be concluded that the cellulose aerogels has a porous structure and good template properties, can be reused after water treatment and can maintain a stable porous structure in water, and this porous structure can promote the adsorption of wastewater (Maatar & Boufi, 2015). At the same time, pure cellulose aerogels do not have a good adsorption capacity for heavy metal ions (Zhu, Yang, Cranston & Zhu, 2016). The cellulose aerogels or cellulose aerogels composites with special functional groups mentioned in the literature have a good adsorption capacity of heavy metal ions (Abbasi, Moradpour & Van Hecke, 2015; He, Cheng, Wang, Zhao, Zhang & Lu, 2014; Maatar & Boufi, 2015).
In this paper, the flexible cellulose aerogels with superior mechanical properties were prepared by alkali/urea as a solvent (Cai, Kimura, Wada, Kuga & Zhang, 2008). The flexible cellulose was used as a template, and MOFs $@$ CA composites were prepared by simple in situ growth at room temperature (Ren, Gao & Lei, 2018). The pre-synthetic CA is soaked in the precursor of the metal ion, and then the organic ligand is added to allow the MOFs to grow on the CA. The performances of
MOFs@CA adsorbing heavy metal ions in water were studied. The MOFs were still functional and the channels were not clogged after the MOFs were grown on the CA by comparing the equilibrium adsorption of heavy metal ions with CA, MOFs, and MOFs@CA composite.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1 Materials
Cotton linter was provided by Heze Sanmu Health Materials Co., Ltd. (Shandong, China). NaOH, urea, zirconium n-propoxide, N,N’-methylenebisacrylamide (MBA)， terephthalic acid, 2-aminoterephthalic acid were purchased from Aladdin Biochemical Technology Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China).N,N’-dimethylformamide (DMF, $299.5\%$ ). Methanol (MA) were supplied by Gaojing Fine Chemical Co., Ltd. (Hangzhou, China).
## 2.2 Synthesis
Synthesis of UiO-66 and UiO-66-NH2 precursor. The synthesis method of UiO-66 and UiO-66-NH2 is according to the reference (Hu, Z, G & Zhao, D, 2015). In details, $71~\mathrm{mL}$ of zirconium n-propoxide, $0.051\mathrm{g}$ $\mathrm{(0.158\mmol)}$ ) of acetone, 7 mL of DMF and 4ml of acetic acid were added to a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ polytetrafluoroethylene reactor, sonicated for 5 min and then allowed to stand in a $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ oven for $10\mathrm{{h}}.$ , to prepare a pale yellow precursor solution.
Synthesis of UiO-66@CA and UiO-66-NH2@CA Composites (Figure1). Preparation of UiO-66@CA and UiO-66-NH2@CA is slightly modified according to the reference (Ren, Gao & Lei, 2018). The cellulose aerogel was dried in an oven at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ to avoid the absorption of water molecules, and then immersed in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of the precursor solution for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . After that $0.15\mathrm{~g~}$ of an organic ligand (terephthalic acid/2-aminoterephthalic acid) was added to the mixture, and stirred for $18\mathrm{~h~}$ . Lastly, the prepared composite aerogels is placed in a beaker with $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol and stirred for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ , then the solution was replaced with fresh methanol, and this process repeated up to five times, then the washed composite aerogels was kept at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ to obtain UiO-66@CA and UiO-66-NH2@CA Composites. Photo of UiO-66@CA and UiO-66-NH2@CA composites see in figure S2.
## 2.3 Characterization
## 2.4 Adsorption experiment
The adsorption experiment was carried out as following: firstly, $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of four kinds solution (concentration $100\mathrm{ppm},$ ) of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{6+}$ were added into four $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ erlenmeyer flask, and $20~\mathrm{mg}$ of UiO-66 powder was added to the mixture solution and sonicated for $2~\mathrm{min}$ , after that the adsorption test was starting at various time of 0.5, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 50, and $64\mathrm{~h~}$ . In the end the mixtures solution transferred to centrifugal treatment at $12000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ , with AAS measurement of metal ion concentration. The adsorption amounts of UiO-66-NH2, CA, UiO- $66@$ CA and UiO-66-NH2@CA were measured under the same conditions.
The solution was analyzed by AAS to determine the concentration based on a calibration curve prepared from solutions with known contaminant concentrations. The adsorption capacity at time t was calculated using the following equation:
$$
\begin{array}{r}{q t=\frac{(C_{0}-C_{T})V}{m}}\end{array}
$$
where V was the heavy metal ion solution volume, m was the mass of CA, the mass of MOF particles or MOFs@CA, C0 was the initial concentration, $\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{t}}$ was the concentration at time t.
The rate constant of adsorption was determined from the pseudo-first-order equation, which is generally expressed as
$$
\ln({\bf q}_{e}-q_{t})=\ln{\bf q}_{e}-{\bf k}_{1}{\bf t}
$$
where $\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}$ and ${\bf q}_{\mathrm{t}}$ are the adsorption capacities $\mathrm{(mg~g^{-1}}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ) at equilibrium and time t, respectively, and $\mathrm{k_{l}w a s}$ the rate constant of the pseudo-first-order model. The time-dependence of the adsorption was fitted with the pseudo-second-order kinetic model, which was:
$$
\frac{\mathrm{~t~}}{\ensuremath{q_{\mathit{\Pi}_{t}}}}=\frac{1}{\ensuremath{k_{\scriptscriptstyle2}}\ensuremath{q_{\mathit{\Pi}_{e}}}^{2}}+\frac{t}{\ensuremath{q_{\mathit{\Pi}_{e}}}}
$$
Where ${\bf q}_{\mathrm{t}}$ and ${\sf q}_{\mathrm{e}}$ were the adsorption capacities at time t and equilibrium, respectively, and k2was the rate constant of the pseudo-second-order model.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1 SEM analyses
The surface morphology of CA, UiO-66, UiO-66-NH2, UiO-66@CA, UiO-66-NH2@CA was observed by SEM as shown in Figure 2. There are many micron-sized macropores on the CA, and the large surface and the inner wall provide a suitable position for the growth of MOFs. As shown in Figure S3 the specific surface areas of UiO-66 and UiO-66-NH2 are $1251.3\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $800.9~\mathrm{\m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ respectively. Additionally, the porosity of pure CA is $45.7\%$ , and the porosity of UiO-66@CA and UiO-66-NH2@CA is increased to $90.5\%$ and $84.6\%$ , respectively. (Supporting Information Table S3). It is found that UiO-66 is uniformly octahedral structure, with a size of $0.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Figure 2A). Besides, UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is also provided octahedral structure with size of $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Figure 2B). This  result is consistent with that reported in the literature (DeStefano, Islamoglu, Garibay, Hupp & Farha, 2017). The surface morphology of UiO- $66@$ CA and UiO-66-NH2@CA was observed by SEM (Figure 2C and D). As shown in Figure 2C and E, the surface of the CA becomes rough after the MOF is grown on the CA, and the UiO-66 and UiO-66-NH2 are uniformly grown on the pore surface of the CA (Figure 2D, F). The size of UiO-66 and UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ is in the range of $1~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and $200~\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. However, in the composite system of UiO-66@CA and UiO-66- $\mathbf{\partial}\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathbf{CA}$ the size of UiO-66 and
UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ were slightly increased compared with pure UiO-66 and UiO-66-NH2. It is obvious that some UiO-66 is embedded in the CA channel (Figure 2D), indicating that UiO-66 is not simply attached to the surface of the CA, but grown on the CA. It can be demonstrated that, UiO-66 cannot easily removed from the pore surface of CA, confirmed by the adsorption of heavy metal ions in water; UiO-66 can be reused. From above it can be concluded that the UiO-66 and UiO-66- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ were successfully grown on CA at room temperature (also supported by XRD results).
## 3.2 XRD analyses
The synthesized UiO-66@CA and UiO-66-NH2@CA composites were characterized by XRD (Figure 3). It is found that the diffraction peaks of UIO-66 and UIO-66-NH2 are similar to those of the simulated UIO-66 (Figure 3. A). Additionally, pure cellulose showed typical diffraction peaks of cellulose Ⅱ crystal structure (Figure 3. B and C) ( Ren, Gao & Lei, 2018). All the characteristic diffraction peaks of UiO-66 and UiO-66-NH2 ( $2\uptheta=7.6{}^{\circ}$ , $8.7^{\circ}$ , $15.6^{\circ}$ , 17.5°) were observed in the XRD patterns of UiO-66@CA and $\mathrm{JiO-}66{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{CA}$ composite, indicating that UiO-66 and UiO-66-NH2 have been successfully grown on cellulose aerogels surface (Figure 3. B and C). It can be concluded that the obtained result in agreement with the UIO-66 aerogel composite reported in the literature (Zhu, Yang, Cranston & Zhu, 2016).
