## 3.5 Separation and reuse of MOFs $@$ cellulose aerogel composites
It is hoped that a larger amount of adsorption can be achieved by combining different MOF and cellulose in the future. The MOFs $@$ CA have prepared in this work provides an idea for the recycling of MOFs. That is, under the principle of ensuring the stability of MOFs, the MOFs can be grown on different substrates and can be replaced by different carriers (carbon fiber, yeast hollow spheres, etc.), and MOF (ZIF-8, UIO-67, etc.) to achieve different applications, not only the adsorption of heavy metal ions, such as improving the mechanical properties of carbon fiber (Yang, Jiang, Huang, Guo & Shao, 2017). The mechanical properties of CA, UiO-66@CA, and UiO-66@CA-NH2 are illustrated in Figure 8. Compared with CA, the compressive stress of UiO-66 $@$ CA and UiO-66 $@$ CA- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ increased by $52\%$ and $60\%$ , respectively. It can be explained that after MOF is grown on cellulose, it can effectively absorb more external stress (Yang, Jiang, Huang, Guo & Shao, 2017). There is no significant decrease in performance after repeated use of material for 5 times (Figure 7, Supporting Information Figure S5). The resultant MOFs@CA composite can easy recycled without complicated centrifugal filtration or magnetic separation (Chen et al., 2013; Zhang et al., 2013), it is only necessary to remove the CA composite from the wastewater. This easily separated composite aerogel is consistent with related literature reports. (M, Schestakow et al., 2016). Figure S6 (Supporting Information) shows that our base material CA can remain dimensionally stable after repeated use, and the internal network structure is not destroyed. The UiO-66-NH2 showed good stability and maintains their original shape, and can be reused for more than 5 cycles without a significant decrease in crystallinity (Supporting Information Figure S5). So, absorbent sustainability is achieved. What's more, the composite aerogels can be easily separated without secondary pollution. Therefore, such excellent reproducibility, reusability, and easy superstation procedure, are making these composite aerogels ideal materials for decontamination of heavy
metal ions in practical applications.
## 4. Conclusions
MOFs@CA composites were prepared by in situ growth and MOFs was successfully grown on the pore surface of CA, which provided new way for the recycling of MOF. The MOFs $@$ CA composites showed good shape stability after recycled as original shape, and can be reused for more than 5 cycles without significant reduction in performance. It is found that, compared with CA the compressive stress of UiO-66@CA and UiO-66 $@$ CA-NH2 increased by $52\%$ and $60\%$ , respectively. After growth of UiO-66-NH2 on the surface of CA aerogel, the thermal stability was improved. Indeed the maximum decomposition temperature $\mathrm{(T_{max})}$ and the complete decomposition temperature $\mathrm{(T_{f})}$ were increased by $20.7^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and $60.7^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for UiO-66@CA, and $24.6\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , $62.1^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for UiO-66-NH2@CA are increased by $26.7~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $62.1\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for UiO-66 $\mathsf{I}{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{A}$ . It is interesting to mention that the obtained MOFs $@$ CA composites can be easily separated without any secondary pollution. The equilibrium adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ adsorbed by UiO-66-NH2@CA was $89.40~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ . Such excellent repeatability, reusability, and ease separation process, could make these composites aerogels ideal for decontamination of heavy metal ions in practical applications.
